  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "The quadratic polynomial $f(z)$ has real coefficients, has roots ${z}_{1}=2\\text{i}$ and ${z}_{2}=-2\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $1.$ Find $f(z).$",
  "worked_solution": "Since our quadratic polynomial has roots ${z}_{1}=2\\text{i}$ and ${z}_{2}=-2\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=1,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(z-{z}_{1})(z-{z}_{2}) & =(1)(z-2\\text{i})(z-(-2\\text{i})) \\\\  & =(z-2\\text{i})(z+2\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z-2\\text{i})(z+2\\text{i}) & ={z}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2} \\\\  & ={z}^{2}-4{\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\  & ={z}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)={z}^{2}+4.$",
